# --- end of jump course tr ---

note keepertri Keeper's log: Triangulation | KEEPER'S LOG. Triangulation, in the careful old hand, pinned beside the case with a fix-pin:\n\nThe surveyors were frightened men. They found the Well and they lied about it - three bearings turned a little wrong, so no boat would find the end of them. I understand fear. I have had forty years of it.\n\nBut a lie is a closed door, and Mara is behind it.\n\nI climbed all three rocks. I turned the masts true with my own spanner. Let the pilgrims follow; let them come in their dozens. A captain, a smoker, a girl with a radio: they are slow, but they are coming, and five posts want five pairs of hands.\n\nNext the static on the shoals. The generator there is in pieces; I left them where clever hands can find them. Calling. Calling.\n\n- S.W.
note teotri Teo's review: the camp | TEO'S REVIEW - on a page of the camp's ration book\n\nTHE SURVEY CAMP, TRIANGULATION ROCKS. One star.\n\nThree rocks, three masts, no roof. Boars on the first top who think they own it, and to be fair to the boars, they were here first. The hollow is nice. The hollow is the only nice thing.\n\nHedda says somebody climbed the masts before us and turned them. I said who climbs a mast for fun. Ines said someone who wants us to follow. Then nobody said anything for a while, and the wind did all the talking.\n\nI buried my last good tobacco by the camp so the madman cannot have it. If I do not come back, it is yours. If I do come back, it is still mine.\n\n- T. Varga
note heddatri Hedda's watch | CAPTAIN'S WATCH - Hedda's square capitals, on the back of a survey form\n\nNight at the camp. Three masts round us with their dead red lights, like three old men at a bedside.\n\nSomebody has been here inside the month. The spanner marks on the mast bolts are bright. The boars avoid the camp. And someone left a fire laid for us, dry wood and all, the way you lay one for guests.\n\nI do not like being expected. I have been expected before, by harbour masters and bailiffs, and it never meant a good dinner.\n\nInes sleeps with the radio on her chest. In her sleep she answers it. Teo sleeps with a rock in his hand, in case of boars. I keep the watch, because somebody should, and because I am the one who said south.
note inestri Ines's radio log: the flat | A page of Ines's radio log, under a stone at the edge of the dig field:\n\nNight four. With all three masts awake, the pulse is so clean I can draw it. Three beats, a pause, three beats. And the old man is louder now - he is not under the pulse any more. He is beside it, like someone reading over a shoulder.\n\nHe says a name. Mara. Then calling, calling.\n\nHedda wants to go straight to the Well. I want to know who is talking first. Teo wants breakfast.\n\nThe fix crosses south over the shoals, where the chart shows nothing but static - grey water, a dead generator, a maze of old sheds. That is where the needle goes next, and so do we. - I.
quest title=Three Bearings
quest intro=Three masts on three rock tops, and a camp in the hollow between them. Go down to the camp.
